# The solver: satisfying assignments, splitters, and general solutions.
#
# See demos/README.md for how to run this script.

# The solve command computes one satisfying assignment for the free variables of
# a formula. Starting with the simplest cases:

solve x = 0
solve x = 0 && y = 0

# Untyped variables live in the Tau Boolean algebra by default, which is why the
# assignments above are reported as {F}:tau. Bringing in a constant of another
# Boolean algebra fixes the type of the equation, and of its solution:

solve {a}:sbf x = 0

# Some systems have no solution that can be written with the constants at hand.
# Here x must be neither 0 nor everything, so the solver introduces a splitter, a
# fresh element strictly between the two:

solve x != 0 && x' != 0

# Splitters exist for the Tau Boolean algebra as well, so the same reasoning
# applies when the constants are themselves Tau specifications:

solve {ex a a = 0} x != 0 && {ex b b = 0} x != 0

# A couple of larger systems, mixing Tau constants and several unknowns:

solve {ex x x = 0} a ^ {ex y y = 0} b = 0
solve a x ^ b y = 0

# solve answers with a single assignment. When you want every solution rather
# than one of them, use lgrs, which computes a least general reproductive
# solution: an assignment parametrized by the variables themselves, whose
# instances are exactly the solutions of the equation.

# Compare the two on the same equation. solve picks one witness:

solve {a}:sbf x = 0

# while lgrs describes the whole solution set, here every x below a':

lgrs {a}:sbf x = 0

# When the only solution is a single element, the two agree:

lgrs x:sbf | y:sbf = 0

# Note that lgrs is defined for Boolean equations only, so an equation over a
# bitvector type is rejected. Bitvectors are solved instead by the route shown
# in demo_2.3-solver-bitvectors.tau:

lgrs x:bv[16] + y:bv[16] = {0}:bv[16]

quit
